.\"
.SS reconcile
Remove index rows for snapshots that no longer exist on disk.
.PP
Backup targets are unmounted between runs, so an absent path is normally no
evidence at all that a snapshot is gone. A snapshot is therefore only ever a
prune candidate when its target root is a
.B verified mountpoint;
anything under an unmounted or unrecognised root is skipped and counted, never
pruned. Spans that merely touch a pruned snapshot have their endpoints moved
inward to the nearest surviving snapshot of the same series rather than being
deleted, so a file is never dropped from snapshots that still exist.
.PP
Targets are mounted for the duration of the pass and unmounted afterward.
.B btrdasd reconcile
takes the maintenance interlock without waiting: while a backup, scrub or
other maintenance pass holds it, the command prints
.B Deferred
and exits 0, changing nothing.
.B btrdasd walk
also runs the prune automatically while targets are still mounted, under the
maintenance lock
.B walk
holds; it waits for that lock rather than deferring (see
.BR walk ).
.PP
.B btrdasd reconcile
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-forget\-root
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-repair ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used for target mount points.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be removed without changing anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-forget\-root " PATH"
First drop every index row recorded under
.IR PATH ,
a mount path that is no longer a configured target (one retired by a rename,
for example). Such rows can never be reconciled, because that root is never
mounted again. A
.I PATH
that is a configured target is refused: nothing else is done, the targets are
unmounted, and the command exits nonzero.
.TP
.B \-\-repair
First delete spans whose endpoints name snapshots that do not exist, and the
files left with no span. Needed on an index carrying such rows, because foreign
keys are enforced and they cannot be rewritten. Has no effect with
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
If the repair fails the pass still finishes and unmounts the targets, and the
command then exits nonzero.
.\"
.SS reindex
Re-index every mounted backup target.
.PP
With
.B \-\-rebuild
the existing index is discarded first and rebuilt from scratch. This is the only
repair for two defects that cannot be fixed in place, because both are properties
of rows already written: spans whose endpoints name snapshots that no longer
exist (foreign keys are enforced, so such rows cannot be rewritten at all), and
file rows migrated from schema v1, whose subvolume series cannot be recovered
after the fact. Backup run history and target usage history are preserved \(em
they are operational records, not derived content.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-rebuild
this is an incremental top-up across every mounted target, indexing only
snapshots not already present.
.PP
The targets are mounted for the duration and unmounted afterwards. Like
.BR reconcile ,
it defers (prints
.BR Deferred ,
exits 0) while the maintenance interlock is held.
.PP
.B btrdasd reindex
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-rebuild ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used for target mount points.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
Discard the index and rebuild it. Takes hours on a large target set.
.\"
.SS forget
Delete obsolete snapshots whose series name matches a pattern.
.PP
For snapshots stranded by a subvolume rename: the old
.I snapshot_name
matches no retention rule, so btrbk never prunes them \(em
.B btrbk clean
handles incomplete and garbled snapshots only, and
.B btrbk prune
matches configured names \(em and they accumulate indefinitely.
.PP
.B Refuses outright
if the pattern also matches a series btrbk is still backing up. The live series
are read from
.IR /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf ,
the file btrbk itself uses, rather than re-derived from
.IR config.toml ,
so the guard cannot disagree with reality. Without that file the command exits
with status 2 rather than proceeding unguarded. A refusal, or a pattern that
matches nothing, exits 1.
.PP
The snapshots are found through the index and deleted with
.BR "btrfs subvolume delete" ;
their index rows are then pruned. The targets are mounted for the duration,
and the command defers (prints
.BR Deferred ,
exits 0) while the maintenance interlock is held.
.PP
.B btrdasd forget
.I PATTERN
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-btrbk\-conf
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I PATTERN
Glob over the snapshot series name
.RI ( * / ? ),
for example
.IR Projects\-old\-name .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan without deleting anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-btrbk\-conf " PATH"
btrbk configuration read for the series still being backed up.
Default:
.IR /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf .
.\"
.SS purge
Delete every snapshot containing files matching a path pattern.
.PP
.B A file cannot be removed from inside a backup.
Target snapshots are received read-only subvolumes; deleting a file requires
clearing
.IR ro ,
which permanently destroys the Received UUID that only
.B btrfs receive
can set, leaving a subvolume that looks like a valid replica while its content
may drift. Whole-snapshot deletion is therefore the only sound granularity, and
purge deletes whole snapshots \(em losing those restore points, and forcing a
full re-send of the affected series on the next backup.
.PP
Unlike
.BR forget ,
purge deliberately has no live-series guard: removing a leaked credential means
removing it from the series still being backed up, which is the case it exists
for.
.PP
.B btrdasd purge
.I PATH_PATTERN
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I PATH_PATTERN
SQLite
.B GLOB
over the file path within a snapshot (case-sensitive), for example
.IR *id_rsa .
Only files the index knows about are matched.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan without deleting anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS setup
Interactive setup wizard \(em configure backup sources, targets, and scheduling.
Requires root privileges.
.PP
.B btrdasd setup
.RB [ \-\-modify ]
.RB [ \-\-upgrade ]
.RB [ \-\-uninstall ]
.RB [ \-\-uninstall\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.TP
.B \-\-modify
Re-open the wizard with the current configuration pre-filled.
.TP
.B \-\-upgrade
Regenerate all managed files from the existing configuration
(useful after a binary update), then restart
.B btrdasd\-helper.service
when it is running, so the GUI is served by the new binary. All of it is done
holding setup's two locks (below); the restart may hold them for at most 300
seconds, so no job can start mounting under it. A restart that fails or does
not return in time, or a unit state that cannot be read, is an error (exit 1).
Before regenerating, it removes the backup units an older version's
.B "cmake \-\-install"
left in
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system
and
.I /usr/local/lib/systemd/system
(das\-backup.service, das\-backup\-full.service and their timers), each only
if its bytes are a version this project installed, whatever install prefix its
ExecStart= line names; anything else at those paths (an edited file, a link, a
directory, a copy reached through a linked directory, a file replaced while it
was being checked) is kept and named. A file it cannot remove, or a path it
cannot read, is an error (exit 1), once the rest is done.
On an init system other than systemd the helper is restarted by hand: the files
are upgraded and the upgrade exits 3. Refused while a job holds a lock: exit 75,
nothing written. Done: exit 0.
A binary from before schema 4 cannot read a run whose snapshot
counts are unknown, so keep
.BR btrdasd ,
.B btrdasd\-helper
and the scripts at one version: the first open by a new binary migrates the
database and waits up to 30 seconds behind another writer, then fails.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall
Remove all generated files, disable timers, and optionally remove the database.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall\-all
Remove ALL installed files: generated configs (same as \-\-uninstall), plus
cmake-installed binaries (btrdasd, btrdasd\-gui, btrdasd\-helper), the backup
scripts under
.IR /usr/lib/das\-backup ,
D\-Bus configs, polkit policy, the helper's systemd unit, man page, shell
completions, desktop entry, icon, the FFI library and header that releases up
to 0.7.22.1 installed, and the backup units that releases up to 0.7.22.3
installed under the prefix. Prompts whether to remove the backup database.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Non-interactive mode: skip all prompts and never remove or overwrite the
backup database. Enables unattended installs, upgrades, uninstalls, and
full uninstalls. For install without an existing config, returns an error
(use the interactive wizard first). Useful for scripted deployments and
CI/CD pipelines.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Validate the configuration and dependencies, report issues,
but change nothing. It reports whether the configuration is valid, whether the
mail relay is reachable (when email is enabled), whether every generated file is present, and, for each
attached target, whether udev actually hides its drives from udisks (read back
from
.BR "udevadm info \-\-export\-db" ,
not from the rule file; an exposed target is reported with the fix). It exits 0
whatever it finds, unless the configuration cannot be loaded at all. Takes
precedence over every other flag. A target with no
.B mount_uuid
is reported, with the UUID udev knows for its drive when it is attached and
the line to add to its
.B [[target]]
\(em without one, the CLI and GUI find the target by drive serial only and
verify only that its mount path is a mount point. The setup wizard records
.B mount_uuid
for a new target whose drive is attached;
.B \-\-upgrade
never adds it to an existing config.
.PP
Every mode that writes or removes installed files \(em the wizard,
.BR \-\-modify ,
.BR \-\-force ,
.BR \-\-upgrade ,
.B \-\-uninstall
and
.B \-\-uninstall\-all
\(em first takes
.I /run/das\-backup.lock
and then
.IR /run/das\-maintenance.lock ,
without waiting, holds both until it is done, and lets them go however it ends.
A backup holds the first from its start, also while it waits for the second;
every job that mounts a backup target holds the second. If either is held,
setup writes and removes nothing, says on standard error which lock is held
and, for the second, by whom, and exits 75; run it again once that job has
finished. The questions of the wizard and of an uninstall come first: the locks
are taken once they are answered.
.B \-\-force
reads the configuration only once it holds them;
.B \-\-modify
compares the file with what it read before the wizard opened, and if anything
else wrote it meanwhile it writes nothing and exits 75. While setup holds the
locks, a backup started meanwhile \(em by the timer, too \(em finds
.I /run/das\-backup.lock
held and skips its run.
.PP
Every file setup writes is replaced whole: a new file, given its mode (scripts
0755, any other file the mode it had) and, written by root, the old file's
owner and group, before it is renamed over the old one. A
backup already reading
.I backup\-run.sh
keeps reading the old file to its end, and a write that fails leaves the old
file as it was and names it. The three scripts end with
.BR "main \(dq$@\(dq; exit $?" ,
so once a run is in
.B main
bash reads its file no more.
.B cmake \-\-install
takes neither lock: never run it while a backup runs. CMake 4.4.3 replaces each
file by removing it and creating a new one, executable only once complete, so
a run already going keeps its script, though it calls the new sibling scripts
and
.B btrdasd
for its later steps. A run that starts in that instant mostly fails at once
(systemd: 203/EXEC), but after the exec bash opens the script again by its
path, so it can find the new one still empty and exit 0 having done nothing
(measured: 17 of 7,764 starts made during 2,000 installs).
.\"
.SS config dump\-env
Print shell-sourceable KEY=VALUE pairs from the configuration.
.PP
.B btrdasd config dump\-env
.RB [ \-\-config
