.\"
.SS config edit
Open the configuration file in
.BR $EDITOR
(defaults to
.BR vi ).
.PP
.B btrdasd config edit
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS backup run
Run the full backup pipeline: sync subvolumes, snapshot, send, boot archive, index, and email report.
Before btrbk runs, the same sync step as
.B "btrdasd subvol sync"
adopts new subvolumes and retires vanished ones, and the run then uses the
updated config.toml. A backup started from the GUI runs the same step. A sync
failure does not stop the run; the run goes on with what is already configured,
is recorded as failed in the backup history and the report (which carries the
.B "SUBVOLUME SYNC"
section), and exits non-zero at the end. A target or source volume the run
mounted and cannot unmount again (each is tried five times, two seconds
apart) fails the run the same way: the error reads
.BR "still mounted:"
followed by the paths. Once the targets are mounted and verified, the run
creates the directory each one receives into (the first
.I target_subdirs
entry of each source, or its label), logging each one it creates, on a dry
run too; a directory that cannot be created fails the run. This command does not
expire retired backups
.RB ( backup\-run.sh
does, with
.BR "subvol expire" ).
When
.B [boot].enabled
is set, the boot subvolumes on each non-mirror target are handled by one rule
here and in
.BR backup\-run.sh :
an incremental run
.I creates
a boot subvolume that is missing, from its latest btrbk snapshot, and never
touches one that exists; a full run (or
.BR "backup boot\-archive" )
archives an existing one to
.IB name .archive. timestamp
and replaces it from the latest snapshot. A subvolume with no snapshot name in
btrbk.conf, no declared target directory or no snapshot to build from is a
warning (the run still exits 0, its report reading COMPLETED WITH WARNINGS);
anything that was meant to work and did not \(em a listing that cannot be
read, a failed create, archive or replace, a path whose state cannot be
determined \(em fails the run. The report is always written to
.BR [general].last_report ;
the GUI's Email box decides only whether it is mailed as well.
.PP
If another backup already holds the singleton lock, the command prints that it
declines and exits 0; it waits for a running scrub. A run that could not start
(nothing selected, a label the configuration lacks, or the locks cannot be taken) exits 1, having mounted and run
nothing. A run that began \(em it took the locks \(em and then stopped on a
target's or a source's state (no target would mount, a target or a source
volume is not what it must be, an absent ticked target, btrbk could not be
started) is recorded as a failed run, its counts unknown, and exits 3, as is a
run that ran with any failure: btrbk failing, the subvolume sync, a target left
mounted, a report that could be neither saved nor mailed, or a history row that
could not be written. Everything else exits 0; a warning is still 0. The GUI
has no exit status: it shows the same outcome as a success flag and the
summary line.
.PP
.BR backup\-run.sh ,
which the scheduled units run, follows the same rule: 0 when nothing
failed, 3 when the run began its work and something failed or it aborted on a
target's or a source's state (target verification and btrbk included), 1 only
when it could not start. Its units count 3 as success
.RB ( SuccessExitStatus=3 ),
so a failure the next start would meet again does not restart a whole backup
every ten minutes; the report, the history and the journal carry it. A run
that aborts before its report sends a short one instead, subject
.BR ABORTED ,
and is recorded as failed. A
.I /run/das\-backup.lock
held by another backup is a skip (0); one that cannot be opened or taken
exits 1. A run stopped by a signal \(em HUP, INT, USR1, PIPE, ALRM or TERM,
which
.B systemctl stop
sends \(em exits with that signal's own code, 128 plus its number, and sends no
report; its unit ends failed. Every report's delivery is bounded: a send that
has not finished within 60 seconds costs the report, not the run.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup run
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-full ]
.RB [ \-\-sources
.IR LABELS ]
.RB [ \-\-targets
.IR LABELS ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Preview: takes the locks, mounts and verifies the sources and targets, and runs
the sync as
.BR "subvol sync \-\-dry\-run" ,
but creates, sends, archives and indexes nothing, and neither reports nor
records the run. A missing target directory is still created and logged, as
above.
.TP
.B \-\-full
Run a full backup instead of incremental:
.B "btrbk run"
in place of
.B "btrbk snapshot"
followed by
.BR "btrbk resume" .
Both apply the retention policy.
.TP
.BI \-\-sources " LABELS"
Comma-separated source labels to back up.
btrbk is told to snapshot and send exactly those sources' subvolumes, by
subvolume, so a source that shares a volume with another is not pulled in by it.
A label that is not in the configuration refuses the run before anything is
locked or mounted (exit 1), even beside valid ones.
A value that names nothing real (an empty one) is such a label: the run is
refused, never widened to every source.
Default (flag not given): every source with at least one subvolume that is not manual-only.
.TP
.BI \-\-targets " LABELS"
Comma-separated target labels to send to.
btrbk is told to write to exactly those targets and is not told about the others:
a target left out can be absent without failing the run, and a target named
that btrbk cannot read fails it. A source whose only targets are left out is
skipped, with a warning.
A label that is not a configured target refuses the run before anything is
locked or mounted (exit 1), even beside valid ones; it is never dropped, and
the selection is never widened to every mounted target.
Default (flag not given): all mounted targets.
.\"
.SS backup snapshot
Create snapshots without sending them to targets
.RB ( "btrbk snapshot" ).
Like the other manual
.B backup
commands below, it declines (exit 0) while another backup holds the singleton
lock, and waits for a running scrub.
Like
.BR "backup run" ,
it first syncs
.I btrbk.conf
with the subvolumes on the mounted sources, and stops if the sync fails.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup snapshot
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-sources
.IR LABELS ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-sources " LABELS"
Comma-separated source labels.
Default: all.
.\"
.SS backup send
Send existing snapshots to targets
.RB ( "btrbk resume" ,
which also applies the retention policy).
Like
.BR "backup run" ,
it first syncs
.I btrbk.conf
with the subvolumes on the mounted sources, and stops if the sync fails:
a send of every source and target passes btrbk no filter, so it would
otherwise trust a
.I btrbk.conf
that may be out of date.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup send
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-targets
.IR LABELS ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-targets " LABELS"
Comma-separated target labels.
Default: all mounted.
.\"
.SS backup boot\-archive
Archive boot subvolumes on backup targets: on each non-mirror target, each
subvolume in
.B [boot].subvolumes
is snapshotted read-only to
.IB name .archive. timestamp
and then replaced from its latest btrbk snapshot. Mirror targets, which carry
their own operating system, are skipped. Snapshot names are read from
.IR /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf ;
if it cannot be read, nothing is done and the step fails. It always archives
and replaces, as a full run does, and creates a subvolume that is missing. Each
failure is printed as
.B FAIL
and each warning as
.BR WARN .
Exit status: 0 when nothing failed (a warning is still 0), 3 when it began and
something failed, 1 when it could not start (configuration unreadable, locks
not taken); another backup holding the singleton lock is a decline, exit 0.
Targets that will not mount once the locks are held are a target's state, not a
failure to start: exit 3, as for
.BR "backup run" .
A step that cannot tell whether a target is mounted, a repeated
.B [boot].subvolumes
entry (or one with a
.B .
or
.B ..
path component) and a repeated target label or mount are failures too: the
boot step itself refuses them before any btrfs call, and
.B backup boot\-plan
exits 2 for them (the configuration loader does not).
.PP
.B btrdasd backup boot\-archive
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS backup boot\-plan
Print the boot subvolumes a full run refreshes, one per line, tab-separated:
the subvolume name, the snapshot name btrbk.conf gives it, and its target
subdirectories, comma-joined
.RB ( \-
for no snapshot name or no subdirectory). It mounts nothing.
.B backup\-run.sh
reads it so that the script and the library derive the same names. Exit status
2, with the reason on standard error and nothing on standard output, when a
configuration file cannot be read or a field is one the script could not pass
on: a subvolume name that is empty, is
.BR / ,
or holds whitespace; a snapshot name that is empty, is a lone
.BR \- ,
or holds whitespace; a subdirectory that is empty, is a lone
.BR \- ,
or holds a comma or whitespace.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup boot\-plan
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS backup report
Show the last backup reports from the database.
A snapshot count the run could not take shows as
.B unknown
.RB ( null
with
.BR \-\-json ),
never as 0.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup report
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-limit
.IR N ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Number of recent runs to show.
Default: 10.
.\"
.SS backup record\-run
Record a completed backup run in the backup history. Used by
.BR backup\-run.sh ;
not normally run by hand.
Give both snapshot counts, or
.B \-\-counts\-unknown
when the run could not count them: the history then stores them as unknown
(NULL), and the history views show
.BR unknown .
A count is never made up: one count without the other, or a count with
.BR \-\-counts\-unknown ,
is refused, and a count cannot be negative.
.PP
.B btrdasd backup record\-run
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-success ]
.RB [ \-\-mode
.IR MODE ]
.RB { \-\-snaps\-created
.I N
.B \-\-snaps\-sent
.I N
|
.BR \-\-counts\-unknown }
.RB [ \-\-bytes\-sent
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-duration\-secs
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-errors
.IR TEXT ]
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to SQLite database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.B \-\-success
Record the run as successful; without it the run is recorded as failed.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
.B incremental
or
.BR full .
Default:
.BR incremental .
.TP
.BI \-\-snaps\-created " N"
Number of snapshots created. Required unless
.B \-\-counts\-unknown
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-snaps\-sent " N"
Number of snapshots sent to targets. Required unless
.B \-\-counts\-unknown
is given.
.TP
.B \-\-counts\-unknown
The run could not count its snapshots \(em for
.BR backup\-run.sh ,
.B "btrbk list latest"
failed, listed no field it knows, or the run ended before the counters were
read. Both counts are recorded as unknown. Not with
.B \-\-snaps\-created
or
.BR \-\-snaps\-sent .
.TP
.BI \-\-bytes\-sent " N"
Bytes sent to targets. Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-duration\-secs " N"
Duration in seconds. Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-errors " TEXT"
Error messages, one per line. Default: none.
.\"
.SS restore file
Restore specific files from a snapshot.
.PP
Every
.B restore
command mounts the targets for its duration, after taking the DAS maintenance
lock exactly as
.B walk
does: it waits for the lock, saying who holds it, or with
.B \-\-no\-wait
exits 75 at once. For
.B restore file
and
.BR "restore snapshot" ,
the snapshot must lie under a target's mount point, and the destination
must lie under one of
.B [restore].allowed_roots
in config.toml (default
.I /home
and
.IR /tmp ),
and never under a system path such as
.IR /etc ,
.IR /usr ,
.IR /boot ,
.I /var/lib
or the served roots
.I /srv/http
and
.IR /srv/ftp ,
which no configuration can allow. Both are compared after symlinks are
resolved, before any directory is created; files are written without following
symlinks, and a member path that is absolute or contains a
.I .\&
or
.I ..\&
component is rejected.
.PP
.B btrdasd restore file
.I SNAPSHOT DEST FILE
.RI [ FILE .\|.\|.]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-wait ]
.TP
.I SNAPSHOT
Path to the snapshot directory.
.TP
.I DEST
Destination directory for restored files.
.TP
.I FILE
One or more file paths relative to the snapshot root (at least one required).
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets and for
.BR [restore].allowed_roots .
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
As for
.BR walk .
.\"
.SS restore snapshot
Restore an entire snapshot using btrfs send/receive or recursive copy.
.PP
.B btrdasd restore snapshot
.I SNAPSHOT DEST
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-wait ]
.TP
.I SNAPSHOT
Path to the snapshot directory.
.TP
.I DEST
Destination directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
As for
.BR "restore file" .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
As for
.BR walk .
.\"
.SS restore browse
Browse files in a snapshot directory.
.PP
.B btrdasd restore browse
.I SNAPSHOT
.RB [ \-\-prefix
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-wait ]
.TP
.I SNAPSHOT
Path to the snapshot directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-prefix " PATH"
Optional subdirectory prefix to browse within the snapshot.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml, used to mount the targets.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
As for
.BR walk .
.\"
.SS schedule show
Show the current backup schedule, including next run times.
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule show
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS schedule set
Update schedule settings in config.toml. Only config.toml is written; the
timer units change at the next
.BR "btrdasd setup \-\-upgrade" .
This command does not validate what it writes: check the result with
.B "btrdasd config validate"
before the next backup run.
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule set
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-incremental
.IR TIME ]
.RB [ \-\-full
.IR SCHEDULE ]
.RB [ \-\-delay
.IR MINUTES ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-incremental " TIME"
Incremental backup time in HH:MM format.
.TP
.BI \-\-full " SCHEDULE"
Full backup schedule as
.RI \(dq Day " HH:MM" \(dq,
where
.I Day
is one of
.BR Sun ,
.BR Mon ,
.BR Tue ,
.BR Wed ,
.BR Thu ,
.B Fri
or
.B Sat
(e.g., "Sun 04:00"); a bare
.I HH:MM
means Sunday. A systemd calendar expression such as "Sun *\-*\-* 04:00:00" is
not accepted by validation.
.TP
.BI \-\-delay " MINUTES"
Randomized delay in minutes before starting
.RB ( [schedule].randomized_delay_min ).
.\"
.SS schedule enable
Enable scheduled backups:
.B "systemctl enable \-\-now"
for
.I das\-backup.timer
and
.I das\-backup\-full.timer
(with OpenRC,
.BR "rc\-update add das\-backup" ;
with SysV init, the generated cron entry). The scrub and drift-check timers
are not affected.
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule enable
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS schedule disable
Disable scheduled backups (the same two timers, with
.BR "systemctl disable \-\-now" ).
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule disable
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS schedule next
Show the next scheduled backup time.
.PP
.B btrdasd schedule next
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol list
List all configured subvolumes across all sources. Retired entries are listed
too, and are not marked as retired;
.B "btrdasd config show"
shows their
.B retired
date.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol list
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol add
Add a subvolume to a source. Validates the whole configuration, then saves
config.toml and regenerates
.I /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf
together (each replaced atomically), or changes neither file.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol add
.I SOURCE NAME
.RB [ \-\-manual\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SOURCE
Source label to add the subvolume to.
.TP
.I NAME
Subvolume name (e.g., "@home").
.TP
.B \-\-manual\-only
Mark the subvolume as manual-only, excluding it from automatic backups.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol remove
Remove a subvolume from a source. Validates and saves config.toml and
regenerates btrbk.conf together, as
.BR "subvol add" .
.PP
Removing the entry of a subvolume that still exists does not stop it being
backed up: the next
.B "subvol sync"
(every backup run) adopts it again. To leave a subvolume out, add a pattern
to
.B [subvolumes].exclude
instead. The snapshots the removed entry already made are not expired by
.BR "subvol expire" ,
which only handles retired entries.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol remove
.I SOURCE NAME
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SOURCE
Source label.
.TP
.I NAME
Subvolume name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol set\-manual
Mark a subvolume as manual-only, excluding it from automatic backups.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol set\-manual
.I SOURCE NAME
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SOURCE
Source label.
.TP
.I NAME
Subvolume name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol set\-auto
Mark a subvolume for automatic backups, removing the manual-only flag.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol set\-auto
.I SOURCE NAME
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.I SOURCE
Source label.
.TP
.I NAME
Subvolume name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS subvol sync
Adopt new subvolumes and retire vanished ones. Every
.B backup\-run.sh
run calls this after verifying the source volumes and before btrbk, and
.B "btrdasd backup run"
calls it too, so creating a subvolume is enough to have it backed up.
.PP
Each subvolume that exists on a source volume and is not excluded gets a
.I [[source.subvolumes]]
entry. A nested subvolume joins its nearest configured ancestor's source and
takes that source's targets. One with no configured ancestor goes to the
primary target only, through a per\-volume
.I <source>\-adopted
source. An adopted entry records the date in
.IR adopted .
An entry whose subvolume is gone is marked
.I retired
with the current date (UTC) and leaves btrbk.conf (see
.BR "subvol expire" ).
A retired entry whose subvolume returns is revived.
Sync mounts nothing: the source volumes must already be mounted, as
.B backup\-run.sh
and
.B "btrdasd backup run"
arrange.
.PP
Excluded are: anything under a
.I .snapshots
or
.I .btrbk\-snapshots
directory, the built\-in
.B @tmp
and
.BR @var\-tmp ,
and the patterns in
.I [subvolumes].exclude
(the older
.I [doctor].exclude
is still read and merged). A pattern
.RI ( * / ? ;
.I *
also matches
.IR / )
names the subvolume
.B without
a trailing slash and also covers everything nested under it:
.I @cache
excludes
.I @cache/x
as well, while
.I @cache/
excludes nothing.
.PP
A volume is read only if every device its sources name is a real mountpoint
holding the expected filesystem UUID, mounted at its top level. Otherwise
nothing on that volume is adopted or retired, and the run is marked failed.
config.toml and btrbk.conf are replaced together or not at all.
.PP
.I /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf
is generated. A sync that changes config.toml writes both files together, even
if some volume could not be read. A sync with nothing to change rewrites it
whenever it differs from what config.toml renders to, and says so in its
report, except on a dry run or when the sync failed (a volume was not read):
then the file is left alone and the report says it is out of date. So do every configuration change made from the GUI,
.BR "subvol add" ,
.BR remove ,
.B set\-manual
and
.BR set\-auto ,
and
.BR "btrdasd setup \-\-upgrade" .
A hand edit is lost the next time any of them rewrites it; change config.toml
instead.
.PP
An adopted entry that is nested under a configured subvolume uses that
subvolume's source: its targets, snapshot directory and target subdirectory,
and its manual\-only flag. One with no configured ancestor goes to the volume's
.I <first\-source\-label>\-adopted
source, created on first need, which takes its device and snapshot directory
from the first source on that volume, uses its own label as the target
subdirectory, and sends to the primary target only. Each adopted entry gets an
explicit snapshot name, the name btrbk would have derived made unique across
the whole configuration with
.IR \-2 ,
.IR \-3 ,
and so on; existing names never change. Under
.B backup\-run.sh \-\-dryrun
this step runs as
.B "subvol sync \-\-dry\-run \-\-render\-btrbk\-conf"
into a temporary file, and
.B "btrbk dryrun"
reads that file: the btrbk.conf the real run would have written by then, so a
pending retirement or adoption is previewed as it will happen (the target
directory a new adopted source sends to is created and logged, as the real
run does).
.PP
Prints a
.B "SUBVOLUME SYNC"
report: what was adopted, retired or revived, every exclusion with its
pattern, how many subvolumes inside snapshot trees were skipped on each volume,
whether btrbk.conf was regenerated, and any subvolume that could not be placed.
If the system clock reads before 2026\-01\-01, no retirement is stamped: the
report says so and the sync fails.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol sync
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run
.RB [ \-\-render\-btrbk\-conf
.IR PATH ]]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and write nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-render\-btrbk\-conf " PATH"
Only with
.BR \-\-dry\-run :
write the btrbk.conf the real run would leave in place into
.IR PATH ,
which must already exist as a regular file (it is never created, and a
symlink is refused). config.toml and the real btrbk.conf are not touched.
.PP
Exit status:
.B 0
sync ran and nothing failed;
.B 1
something failed (a volume was not read, a subvolume could not be placed,
the files could not be written, a retirement was refused because of the
clock, or there was no btrbk.conf to render);
.B 2
the configuration could not be loaded.
.\"
.SS subvol expire
Delete the backups of retired subvolumes whose window has passed. Run by
.B backup\-run.sh
directly after btrbk, while the targets are mounted.
.PP
A retired entry's snapshots are looked for in each
.I location:
every target directory its source sends to, and the source's own snapshot
directory. On a target they are kept
until the retirement date plus that target's longest retention window (daily
x1, weekly x7, monthly x31, yearly x366 days), then all deleted from that
target together; until then the report reads
.RI \(dq N " snapshots kept, deleted on or after " YYYY\-MM\-DD \(dq.
On the source side they are kept until the retirement date
plus the shortest of the targets' windows, then deleted. A target with no
retention configured keeps them and says
so. Expiry mounts nothing; a location whose mount point is not mounted is
reported as not reachable and left alone. Nothing is deleted from a location that is not a mountpoint or cannot be
read completely, that is also used by a live entry of the same series name,
or that holds names resembling the series that are not recognised btrbk
snapshots; nor at a location whose newest snapshot of the series is dated
more than a day after the retirement date (that date is wrong). Nothing of an
entry is deleted while a path with its name exists on its mounted source
volume, and nothing at all while the system clock reads before 2026\-01\-01
(reported, exit 1). A clock that wrongly reads far in the future at expiry time
is not detected and would expire retired series early; live subvolumes are
never touched by expiry. Run by hand, it keeps every retired entry whose source
volume is not mounted. When the run's sync failed,
.B backup\-run.sh
runs this command with
.B \-\-dry\-run
only. The entry leaves config.toml only once nothing remains to expire
(never if it is the last entry, or its source sends to no target, and never
on a dry run); a source left with no entries by that leaves with it. The
search index is pruned of the deleted snapshots.
.PP
Prints a
.B "RETIRED SUBVOLUMES"
report, or nothing when no entry is retired.
.PP
.B btrdasd subvol expire
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-db
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.TP
.BI \-\-db " PATH"
Path to the SQLite index database.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print what would be deleted and delete nothing.
.PP
Exit status:
.B 0
ran and nothing failed;
.B 1
something failed, including deleted snapshots that could not be removed from
the index;
.B 2
the configuration could not be loaded.
.\"
.SS health
Show backup system health \(em drive status, SMART diagnostics,
disk usage, and growth trends.
.PP
With a
.B "role = \(dqmirror\(dq"
target configured it also prints a
.B "Recovery OS"
line per mirror target: read live from
.I <mount>/@
while the target is mounted, otherwise the record the last backup run kept in
.IR /var/lib/das\-backup/recovery\-os.json ,
with the time it was taken and the age recomputed for today. Run it as root
while the drives are mounted, or the live read reports the files it may not
read without updating their access times; each line names the timers enabled
in that OS and whether btrbk would run at its boot
.RB ( will ,
.B may
or
.BR no ),
and ends with the number of paths that could not be read, when there are
any. A stale, unreadable or never\-checked recovery OS, and one whose boot
will or may run btrbk (see
.BR "recovery\-os inspect" ),
is also a warning, so the status reads
.BR WARNING .
.PP
.B btrdasd health
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS recovery\-os inspect
Read one recovery OS root and print its
.B "RECOVERY OS"
block. Every
.B "role = \(dqmirror\(dq"
target carries its own bootable install under
.I @
on the filesystem that receives the backups; this reads it and never writes
to it. Read: the name in
.IR etc/os\-release ,
the kernels under
.IR usr/lib/modules ,
the install date and the dates of the last full system upgrade attempted
and the last one applied from
.I var/log/pacman.log
(the install date is that of the log's first line \(em pacman's first entry
on an install is its
.B pacman \-b
from the live ISO \(em and is unknown when that line carries no date; an
attempt is a
.RB \(dq "starting full system upgrade" \(dq
line, logged before anything is downloaded or verified; it was applied only if
.RB \(dq "[ALPM] transaction completed" \(dq
follows before the next pacman invocation starts),
and the installed versions of btrfs\-progs, btrbk, linux\-cachyos and
das\-backup\-manager from
.I var/lib/pacman/local
(matched by the package's own name field). Not even an access time is
written: files and directories are opened with
.BR O_NOATIME ,
which needs root or ownership of the file (without it the item is reported
as unreadable rather than read), and a symlink inside the root is never
followed or read on a mount that would record the access, since doing so
updates the link's own access time (the boot reading below follows links
only where the mount is
.B noatime
or read\-only). So
.I etc/os\-release
is read from
.IR usr/lib/os\-release ,
the fallback
.BR os\-release (5)
names, when it is a link. A root that is itself a symlink is refused, and a
file that is not a regular file is not opened.
Whatever is there but cannot be read prints as
.B unknown
and is listed under
.BR "Could not read" ;
whatever is not there at all is said to be absent. A package missing from a
readable database prints as
.BR "not installed" .
.PP
It also reads what that OS starts at boot, to say whether booting it \(em on
bare metal or in the update VM \(em would run btrbk. A walk starts where
systemd starts,
.B default.target
.RI ( etc/systemd/system/default.target ,
the link
.B "systemctl set\-default"
makes, else the vendor's), and takes all it pulls in as starting at every
boot. From each unit the walk goes on to what it starts: the units its
.B [Unit]
section pulls in with
.BR Wants= ,
.BR Requires= ,
.BR Requisite= ,
.BR BindsTo= ,
.BR Upholds= ,
.B OnSuccess=
or
.B OnFailure=
(in its file or a drop\-in \(em an empty one of these resets nothing, as
.BR systemd.unit (5)
says), the names in its
.IR UNIT .wants ,
.IR UNIT .requires
and
.IR UNIT .upholds
directories, the unit a timer or a path unit names
.RB ( Unit= ,
else the one of its own name), and the service a socket starts when
something connects to it
.RB ( Service= ,
else the one of its own name; for
.BR Accept=yes ,
its template). Then it does the same from every name in every such directory
of the unit trees \(em what
.B systemctl enable
and packages enable \(em for what the boot did not reach. The trees are
systemd's persistent unit path, highest first:
.IR etc/systemd/system.control ,
.IR etc/systemd/system ,
.IR etc/systemd/system.attached ,
.I usr/local/lib/systemd/system
and
.IR usr/lib/systemd/system .
Each unit is read once, by name, in that order \(em an instance falls back to
its template \(em with its drop\-ins: those in its own, its template's, its
dash prefixes' and its type's
.I .d
directories, each file name once, applied in file name order, an empty
assignment clearing an
.B Exec*=
list. The specifiers that come from a unit's own name
.RB ( %i ,
.BR %n ,
.BR %N ,
.BR %p ,
.BR %j ,
.BR %% )
are resolved in the names it gives and in its commands \(em but a template's
own instance, a connection's, is unknown \(em and a template left over is
instantiated as systemd does; a name with any other specifier is unknown. A
unit reached again sooner than before passes that on to what it starts; at
most 4096 units are read and 262144 starts followed (more is unknown), and
each way a unit is started is reported.
.PP
