.SS recovery\-os hold\-disk
An internal helper, not listed by
.BR \-\-help :
.B recovery\-os\-vm.sh
starts it for the length of a session in which a recovery drive is lent to
the
.B recovery\-os\-updater
virtual machine. It opens the whole disk read\-only with
.BR O_EXCL ,
an exclusive claim on the block device. While the claim stands, the kernel
refuses to mount any partition of that disk on the host \(em by device or by
UUID, in any mount namespace \(em while the virtual machine's own
non\-exclusive open of the disk still works. Two kernels mounting one BTRFS
filesystem corrupt it; this is what keeps the host's out. Conversely, a disk
with any partition mounted cannot be claimed, so a hold that succeeds also
proves that nothing on the host has the drive mounted.
.PP
Once the claim is in place it prints one line,
.IR "held PATH pid PID" ,
flushed, and then waits for
.BR SIGTERM ,
.B SIGINT
or
.B SIGHUP
\(em blocked first, then given back their default disposition, so a parent
that ignored one (a shell does SIGINT for every background job) cannot make
it deaf to it. On one of them it closes the disk \(em the claim ends \(em says so on
standard error and exits 0. It never reads standard input and never writes
standard output again, so it does not end when the process that started it
dies or closes its pipes. Ctrl\-C or a hangup in a terminal does reach it,
though, unless it was started in a session of its own; the script starts it
with
.BR setsid (1),
in a transient systemd scope of its own
.RB ( "systemd\-run \-\-scope" ,
.IR das\-recovery\-os\-holder\- LABEL .scope ,
outside the operator's login session, which can end without ending it), and
lets it inherit its descriptor of
.IR /run/das\-maintenance.lock ,
so that the claim and the lock both outlive the script while the virtual
machine may still be running. The lock's record then names this process, the
one that holds it. A
.B SIGKILL
ends the claim too: the kernel drops it with the descriptor.
.PP
The claim says nothing about whether the recovery OS boots. When the virtual
machine's firmware tries a network (PXE) boot or stops in its UEFI shell, the
disk did not boot; the firmware can be pointed at the loader on the drive's
ESP by hand, as
.I docs/DISASTER\-RECOVERY\-GUIDE.md
("In the recovery\-os\-updater VM") describes.
.PP
.B btrdasd recovery\-os hold\-disk
.B \-\-device
.I PATH
.TP
.BI \-\-device " PATH"
Any block device (a symbolic link is followed); anything else is refused. It
does not check that the device is a whole disk \(em
.B recovery\-os\-vm.sh
passes only a whole disk, the drive's
.I /dev/disk/by\-id/ata\-MODEL_SERIAL
link, and checks that itself.
.PP
.B \-\-json
is refused: the output is one plain line for a script to read.
.PP
Exit status:
.B 0
released on one of those signals;
.B 2
refused \(em the path is not a block device (it is never opened then: an
open of a FIFO would block), the disk is in use
.RB ( EBUSY :
mounted, or held by another program), any other open error, the device
behind the path changed while it was being opened, the hold could not be
announced on standard output, or
.BR \-\-json .
.\"
.SS scrub run
Run a full scrub pass now: acquire locks, mount each configured scrub
target by filesystem UUID, run
.BR "btrfs scrub start \-B" ,
parse the result, unmount, persist state, and email a report \(em the
same path the scheduled systemd timer uses. A filesystem whose last scrub was
interrupted, with nothing scrubbing it now, is resumed with
.B "btrfs scrub resume \-B"
instead, falling back to
.B "btrfs scrub start \-B \-f"
when there is nothing to resume. The targets are scrubbed one at a time, in
.B [scrub].targets
order.
.PP
If another scrub pass holds
.IR /run/das\-scrub.lock ,
this one is skipped (exit 0). The shared maintenance lock is waited for, so a
pass started during a backup begins when the backup ends.
.PP
This runs even when
.B [scrub].enabled
is
.B false
in config.toml: that flag only gates whether the
.I scheduled
timer fires, never a direct invocation of this command. A manual run is
exactly the intended use of a temporarily-disabled schedule, and a
warning is printed to make that explicit rather than silent.
.PP
.B Exit code:
0 means the pass
.I ran
(scrubbing was attempted on at least one target), regardless of what it
found \(em per-FS errors, aborted scrubs, and unmount problems are
reported via the FAILURE email and
.B "btrdasd health"
Critical escalation, never via this exit code. Nonzero means the pass
could
.I not
run at all: a config load failure, a lock-file I/O error, or every
target failing before any scrub was attempted. This split (bd
.BR DAS\-Backup\-Manager\-18p )
exists so a Sentinel-monitored systemd unit never retry-loops a real
multi-hour scrub over failing hardware just because it found damage
\(em only a genuine can't-even-start failure, which fails in seconds,
trips the unit into
.B failed
state.
.PP
.B btrdasd scrub run
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS scrub status
Show the last scrub result for every configured scrub target.
.PP
Resolved by filesystem UUID, never by mount path, so this works while
the DAS filesystems are unmounted. Reads the engine's persisted state
.RI ( /var/lib/das\-backup/scrub\-state.json )
when an entry exists, falling back to the raw btrfs record
.RI ( /var/lib/btrfs/scrub.status. <fsuuid> )
for a filesystem with scrub history predating this CLI or the state
file. A target with neither source is reported as
.BR "NEVER SCRUBBED" ,
not an error.
.PP
.B btrdasd scrub status
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS scrub cancel
Cancel the filesystem currently being scrubbed.
.PP
Manual operator action only \(em the scrub interlock never cancels a
pass automatically. Locates the actively-scrubbing filesystem via the
engine's lock and live kernel state, then issues
.B "btrfs scrub cancel"
against it. A no-op (clean exit, no error) when no scrub is running. Exits 1
if the cancel fails.
.PP
.B btrdasd scrub cancel
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS doctor
Subvolume drift detector \(em find source subvolumes that exist on disk but
aren't backed up, and configured subvolumes that no longer exist on disk.
.PP
For each configured
.I [[source]]
volume (deduplicated \(em multiple sources commonly share one physical
volume), mounts it if needed, runs
.BR "btrfs subvolume list" ,
and compares the result against every
.I [[source]].subvolume
name configured for that volume. The listing is the one
.B "subvol sync"
uses: a volume is listed only if every device its sources name is a real
mountpoint holding the expected filesystem UUID at its top level, and an empty
listing counts as a failure. Built-in exclusions (never configurable)
skip anything under a
.I .snapshots/
or
.I .btrbk\-snapshots/
directory at any depth, and the
.B @tmp
/
.B @var\-tmp
ephemeral subvolumes. The
.I [subvolumes].exclude
list, and the older
.I [doctor].exclude
(both shell-style glob patterns
.RI ( * / ? ),
merged), add further exclusions on top; the same rules as
.BR "subvol sync" ,
including that a pattern names a subvolume without a trailing slash and covers
everything nested under it.
.PP
Missing subvolumes (on disk, not configured by a live entry, not excluded)
are reported in one section,
.BR "NOT BACKED UP" ,
because the backup run adopts new subvolumes itself
.RB ( "subvol sync" ):
a finding means that step failed or has not run since the subvolume appeared,
and the report advises
.BR "btrdasd subvol sync \-\-dry\-run" .
A retired entry is neither configured nor stale. Stale config entries
(configured, not retired, no longer on disk) are reported separately.
.PP
.B Locking:
non-blocking only \(em a drift check that finds either its own singleton lock
.RI ( /run/das\-doctor.lock )
or the shared maintenance lock
.RI ( /run/das\-maintenance.lock ,
held by a running backup or scrub) already taken defers immediately rather
than waiting, since a fast read-mostly scan has nothing urgent enough to
delay a real backup or scrub for.
.PP
.B Exit code:
.B 0
means no drift was found, or the check deferred because a lock was held
(never treated as a failure).
.B 1
means drift was found (missing or stale subvolumes). Unlike
.BR "btrdasd scrub run" ,
this genuinely surfaces through the exit code, because a drift check has no
Sentinel multi-hour-retry hazard to protect against.
.B 2
means the check could not run at all: a config load failure, a lock I/O
error, or every configured source volume failed to mount or list (nothing
was ever examined).
.B 3
means at least one configured volume failed to mount or list while others
were checked (that volume's subvolumes went unchecked), or a volume the check
mounted could not be unmounted again (listed under
.BR "LEFT MOUNTED" ).
It outranks 1: an incomplete check cannot claim its drift list is complete.
.PP
.B btrdasd doctor
.RB [ \-\-check\-drift ]
.RB [ \-\-email ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.TP
.B \-\-check\-drift
Run the subvolume drift check. Currently the only check this command
performs \(em the flag exists so future checks can be selected individually
without a breaking CLI change. Omitting it still runs the drift check today.
.TP
.B \-\-email
Email a report via the configured SMTP settings, but only when drift or an
error was found \(em a clean run stays silent even with this flag, so the
weekly timer doesn't send an all-clear every Sunday.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to config.toml.
Default:
.IR /etc/das\-backup/config.toml .
.\"
.SS completions
Generate shell completions.
.PP
.B btrdasd completions
.I SHELL
.TP
.I SHELL
Shell to generate completions for. One of
.BR bash ,
.BR elvish ,
.BR fish ,
.BR powershell ,
or
.BR zsh .
.\"
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Produce machine-readable JSON output on all read commands.
This flag is global and may be placed before any subcommand.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit. The version printed is the Rust crate's
three-part version (for example
.B 0.7.23
for release 0.7.23.0).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful operation. Also: a command that found its lock held and deferred or
declined
.RB ( backup ,
.BR reconcile ,
.BR reindex ,
.BR forget ,
.BR purge ,
.BR doctor ,
.BR "scrub run" ),
and a scrub pass that ran, whatever it found.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (backup failure, validation error, missing configuration, a
mount point the command mounted and could not unmount again, a
.B "setup \-\-upgrade"
whose helper restart failed, etc.).
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed. Also used by
.B forget
(no readable btrbk.conf),
.B "subvol sync"
and
.B "subvol expire"
(configuration could not be loaded),
.B recovery\-os
(could not be checked; 1 there means a recovery OS needs attention \(em it is
stale, or btrbk may run when it boots), and
.B doctor
(could not run).
.TP
.B 3
.BR "btrdasd doctor" :
the check ran but some volume went unexamined; see its section.
.BR "btrdasd setup \-\-upgrade" :
the files were upgraded, but
.B btrdasd\-helper
is yours to restart \(em the init system is not systemd, so setup can neither
tell whether one runs nor restart it. This is not a failed install.
.TP
.B 75
.B walk
or a
.B restore
command run with
.B \-\-no\-wait
found the DAS maintenance lock held and deferred: nothing was mounted or
changed.
.B "btrdasd setup"
in any mode that writes or removes installed files found
.I /run/das\-backup.lock
or the DAS maintenance lock held \(em a backup or another job was running \(em
or, with
.BR \-\-modify ,
found the configuration file changed while the wizard was open: nothing was
written or removed. (EX_TEMPFAIL: try again later.)
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
Editor for
.BR "config edit" .
Default:
.BR vi .
.TP
.BR DAS_REPORT_TO ", " DAS_REPORT_FROM
Override the report recipient and sender address
.RB ( [email].to ,
.BR [email].from )
for the emails this program sends. Meant for testing: the sender selects the
mail relay's upstream credential.
.TP
.BR DAS_SCRUB_STATE ", " DAS_BTRFS_STATUS_DIR
Override the scrub state file and the directory of btrfs scrub status
records. Meant for tests only: they are read only by a test build, and a
shipped binary ignores them.
.TP
.B DAS_MAINTENANCE_LOCK_FD
The number of an open descriptor on
.I /run/das\-maintenance.lock
through which the calling process holds the lock.
.B walk
and the
.B restore
commands then use that hold instead of waiting for the lock, which would never
end: the caller holds it until the command finishes.
.B backup\-run.sh
sets it for the
.B walk
it runs. It is checked, not trusted: the descriptor must be that file, the lock
must be held, and it must be held through that descriptor \(em or the command
fails. A descriptor on a free lock is refused rather than allowed to take it.
If the real holder lets go at the very moment of the check, a descriptor that
held nothing may still end up holding the lock: its process then holds it,
with no holder recorded, until it exits.
.TP
.B DAS_RECOVERY_OS_STATE
Override the recovery OS record
.B btrdasd health
reads, and the one
.B backup\-run.sh
writes. Meant for tests only.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a full backup pipeline:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd backup run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview a backup without making changes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd backup run \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a full (non-incremental) backup of specific sources to specific targets
(labels as configured in config.toml):
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd backup run \-\-full \-\-sources nvme,ssd \-\-targets primary\-22tb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview which subvolumes the next backup would adopt or retire:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd subvol sync \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview what expiry of retired subvolumes would delete (targets must be
mounted):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd subvol expire \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search for files matching a prefix across all indexed snapshots:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd search 'report*'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search with JSON output and limited results:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd \-\-json search 'config' \-\-limit 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Browse a snapshot directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd restore browse /mnt/backup\-22tb/nvme/home.20260228T0300
.fi
.RE
.PP
Restore specific files from a snapshot (the destination must be under
.BR [restore].allowed_roots ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd restore file /mnt/backup\-22tb/nvme/home.20260228T0300 /tmp/restored user/.bashrc user/.config/kdeglobals
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show drive health and backup status:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd health
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show backup schedule and next run times:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd schedule show
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show the last scrub result for every configured DAS filesystem:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd scrub status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Cancel a scrub currently in progress:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd scrub cancel
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check for subvolume drift (source vs config) and email a report if found:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd doctor \-\-check\-drift \-\-email
.fi
.RE
.PP
List all configured subvolumes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd subvol list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Index snapshots on a backup target:
.PP
.RS
.nf
btrdasd walk /mnt/backup\-22tb
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run the interactive setup wizard:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd setup
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check the installation, including whether the targets are hidden from udisks
and which targets lack a
.BR mount_uuid :
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd setup \-\-check
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non-interactive full uninstall (preserves the backup database):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd setup \-\-uninstall\-all \-\-force
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non-interactive reinstall from existing config:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo btrdasd setup \-\-force
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/backup\-index.db
SQLite database with FTS5 content index and backup history.
.TP
.I /etc/das\-backup/config.toml
Main configuration file for sources, targets, schedule, and email settings.
.TP
.I /etc/btrbk/btrbk.conf
btrbk configuration file, generated from config.toml by
.BR "btrdasd setup" ,
.B "subvol sync"
(every backup run), the
.B subvol
commands that change an entry, and the GUI. Do not edit it by hand.
.TP
.I /etc/das\-backup/.manifest
The files
.B btrdasd setup
generated, one per line; read by
.B \-\-check
and
.BR \-\-uninstall .
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/99\-das\-backup\-udisks\-ignore.rules
Generated udev rule that hides the backup targets from udisks, matched on the
targets' drive serials and
.BR mount_uuid .
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/growth.log
Disk usage growth log for trend monitoring, appended by
.B backup\-run.sh
and read by
.BR "btrdasd health" .
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/last\-report.txt
The last backup report, written before it is emailed
.RB ( [general].last_report ).
.TP
.IR /run/das\-backup.lock ", " /run/das\-scrub.lock ", " /run/das\-reconcile.lock ", " /run/das\-doctor.lock
Singleton locks: one backup, scrub, reconcile pass (also taken by
.B reindex
and
.BR forget / purge )
or drift check at a time.
.TP
.I /run/das\-maintenance.lock
Shared maintenance lock held by every job that mounts the backup targets \(em
backups, scrubs,
.BR walk ,
.BR restore ,
.BR reconcile ,
.BR reindex ,
.BR forget ,
.BR purge ,
the drift check, and the GUI's index and restore jobs \(em so they never
overlap. Backups, scrubs,
.B walk
and
.B restore
wait for it (a GUI job stops waiting when cancelled); the others defer.
Whoever holds it writes one line into it naming itself, such as
.BR "btrdasd restore browse pid 4242" ,
and empties it again on release.
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/scrub\-state.json
Persisted scrub state, keyed by filesystem UUID (mode 644). Absent until
the first
.B btrdasd scrub run
completes.
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/recovery\-os.json
What the last backup run read from each recovery drive's own OS, keyed by
target label (mode 644). Written by
.BR "recovery\-os status \-\-state\-file" ,
read by
.B "btrdasd health"
and by
.BR recovery\-os\-vm.sh ,
which starts a session only when the drive's entry names the filesystem
config mounts that target by
.RB ( mount_uuid )
and does not say btrbk will run when its OS boots.
.TP
.I /var/lib/das\-backup/recovery\-os\-vm\-sessions
When
.B recovery\-os\-vm.sh
last booted each recovery drive's OS in the virtual machine, one
.I "LABEL SECONDS"
line per target label (mode 644): written before the OS boots and again when
the disk is given back. A boot record made before that time no longer counts.
.TP
.I /run/das\-recovery\-os\-vm/
Kept by
.B recovery\-os\-vm.sh
while a recovery drive is lent to the
.B recovery\-os\-updater
virtual machine (mode 700): per target label, the
.B "recovery\-os hold\-disk"
process's pid, the disk it holds and its scope
.RI ( LABEL .holder),
that process's output, and the disk definition attached. Removed when the
disk is given back.
.TP
.I /var/lib/btrfs/scrub.status.<fsuuid>
Raw btrfs scrub status record for one filesystem, written by btrfs\-progs.
Read directly by
.B btrdasd scrub status
as a fallback when no state-file entry exists.
